  "worked_solution": "Let $W$ be the event that the chosen apple has a worm inside, and let $R$ be the event that the chosen apple is ripe.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(W)=P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{\u2032})P({R}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $80%$ of the apples are ripe and $20$ are unripe, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.8,\\,P({R}^{\u2032})=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6,$ and the probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|R)=0.6,\\,P(W|{R}^{\u2032})=0.1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{\u2032})P({R}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 0.8+0.1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.48+0.02 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
